          %h2#Summary Summary
          %p The text starts with showing or recalling that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the collection of all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
                theories and
            %li
              %p
                the gluing of their representing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
                into the single
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
                spectrum
          %p
            is best understood in terms of global sections of the structure sheaf of functions on the refinement of the moduli
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            of all elliptic curves to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            Then it uses this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            perspective to analyze further properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            theories, in particular their refinements to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
          %h3#gluingallellipticcohomologytheoriestothetmfspectrum Gluing all elliptic cohomology theories to the tmf spectrum
          %p
            The triple of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theories
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                periodic ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %li
              %p
                complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
          %p
            constitutes the collection of all possible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theories with the extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} property
            that they are
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+cohomology+theory"}> periodic
              \.
          %p
            It so happens that all multiplicative periodic generalized Eilenberg-Steenrod cohomology theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are characterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group"} formal group
            (an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            of functions is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+ring"} cohomology ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\mathbb{C}P^\infty)
            obtained by evaluating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on the complex projective space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty \simeq \mathcal{B} U(1)
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            for complex
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty \times \mathbb{C}P^\infty \to \mathbb{C}P^\infty
            that representes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of complex
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
          %p There are precisely three different types of such formal groups:
          %ul
            %li
              %p the additive formal group (a single one)
            %li
              %p the multiplicative formal group (a single one)
            %li
              %p
                a formal group defined by an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
                (many).
          %p
            The first case corresponds to periodic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
          %p
            It is therefore natural to subsume all elliptic cohomology theories into one single cohomology theory. This is the theory called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
          %p
            It turns out that the right way to formalize what “subsume” means in the above sentence involves formulating the way in which an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            theory is associated to a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            in the correct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            language:
          %p
            The collection of all 1-dimensional
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{1,1}
            – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            – defined by the property that it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            for elliptic curves in that elliptic curves over a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            correspond to classifying maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : Spec R \to M_{1,1}
          %p
            Then the classical assignment of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            theory to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            is an assignment
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mi CohomologyTheories
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\phi : Spec R \to M_{1,1}\}
                  \\to
                  CohomologyTheories
                  \\,.
          %p
            We may think of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec R \to M_{1,1}
            as picking certain subsets of the generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{1,1}
            and of morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Spec
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Spec
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi R
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mrow
                              %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Spec(R) &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; Spec(R')
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; M_{1,1}
                  }
          %p
            as maps between such subsets. Hence the assignment of cohomology theories to elliptic curves is much like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of cohomology theories on the moduli
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"}> stack
            ) of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
          %p
            In order to
            %em glue
            all elliptic cohomology theories in some way one would like to take something like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            of this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
          %p
            As emphasized at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            that represents it. It is and was well known how to do this for each elliptic curve separately. What is not so clear is how this can be done coherently for all elliptic curves at once: we need a lift of the above cohomology-theory-valued sheaf to a sheaf of representing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Spectra
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mo ?
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi represent
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo :
                          %mi Spec
                          %mi R
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mrow
                              %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi CohomologyTheories
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Spectra
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{?}\nearrow &amp; \;\;\;\downarrow^{represent}
                  \\\
                  \\{\phi : Spec R \to M_{1,1}\}
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  CohomologyTheories
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this generality this turns out to be a hard problem. But by definition here we are really interested just in the special case where all cohomology theories in question are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative cohomology theories
            and where hence all spectra in question are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi CommRingSpectra
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi O
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mi M
                                    %mi der
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo :
                          %mi Spec
                          %mi R
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mrow
                              %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi MultiplicativeCohomologyTheories
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; CommRingSpectra
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{O_{M^{der}}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\{\phi : Spec R \to M_{1,1}\}
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  MultiplicativeCohomologyTheories
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            As indicated, this problem does turn out to have a solution:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"} Goerss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Hopkins
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Miller
            showed that the desired lift denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mi der
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_{M^{der}}
            above exists – the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goerss-Hopkins-Miller+theorem"} Goerss-Hopkins-Miller theorem
          %p
            Accordingly, one can then obtain the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            spectrum as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            of this sheaf of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi M
                          %mi der
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_{M^{der}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} limit in a quasi-category
            that such a homotopy limit computes global sections. It is an
            = succeed "-version" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
          %h3#interpretationintermsofhighertopostheory Interpretation in terms of higher topos theory
          %p
            What is noteworthy about the above construction is that, as the notation above suggests, sheaves of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+ring"} E-infinity ring
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+space"} ringed space
            = succeed "." do
              %strong structure sheaves
          %p
            Accordingly, the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mi der
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_{M^{der}}
            makes the moduli
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            of elliptic curves into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
          %p
